From 09935448aa3bc4db8e23a8b293c31d38920a3b2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 01:58:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/27] extract: resolve the tier's credential from vault
 custody, not the env alone
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

P1.T2's custody rule had one source implemented (the environment) and the spec
names two. `resolve_api_key` now takes both lookups explicitly — environment
first (an explicit per-run override), then `crate::vault::credential` under the
entry `tallow-llm-api-key`.

The signature takes the lookups as closures rather than reading them inline, so
precedence and the refusal are testable without touching the process environment
or a real vault (edition 2024 makes env mutation unsafe, which is the right nudge:
a testable pure function beats a test that mutates global state).

A blank value from either source is not a credential, and the refusal names BOTH
sources it looked at, so "not configured" and "looked and found nothing usable"
are distinguishable at the failure site.

Recorded trade-off: `Credential::password` is `Zeroizing<String>`; returning a
plain `String` drops zeroize-on-drop for the copy that lives for the duration of
the request. That copy is unavoidable once the value becomes an HTTP header — the
alternative is threading Zeroizing through reqwest's header API — so it is stated
rather than implied.

Verified: cargo check --lib --bins RC=0; engine_tests 16 passed/0 (env-only
refusal, vault consultation, env-over-vault precedence, a blank value from either
source, and a refusal naming both sources); llm_extract 46, smart_extract 16 and
llm_transfer 7, all passing.

argv check, CORRECTED after the first attempt lied: with the credential in the
process environment, a snapshot of every process's argv (`ps -eo pid,ppid,args`)
contains no key material, and `src/main.rs` has no credential-bearing flag. The
first version of this check reported "LEAK" — it was wrong: it piped `ps -ef` into
`rg <value>`, whose OWN command line carries the pattern, so it matched itself. A
focused probe shows the difference (snapshot-search: none; naive pipe: 1), which
is why the check now searches a snapshot file instead of a live pipe.
